    "number": 238,
    "title": "Retire ended Codex Desktop tasks and reset resumed monitoring",
    "body": "Refs #218\nCompanion: https://github.com/jimmie-potts/codex-nanoleaf/pull/116\n\nAn ended Codex Desktop session currently keeps its shared monitoring task and device assignments. This change retires the known parent and descendants in one durable revision. Bounded retirement records reject recognizable delayed events; eligible resumed work gets a fresh generation. Existing 24-hour expiry and other provider/client policies remain intact. Archive filename evidence is checked only during new admission, including known ancestors, using the existing configured Codex home.\n\nSnapshot 1.0 remains the default. Opt-in 1.1 adds generation metadata for consumers that must distinguish a recreated identity after missed removal. Agent State 3.0.0 / Hub 0.3.0 use durable format 2.0 and import 1.0 without resetting evidence clocks. Older owners cannot read new stores; see `docs/session-retirement-compatibility.md` for installation order and rollback limits.\n\nFocused checks demonstrated two consumer gaps: Nanoleaf retained manual task projects/effects after missed removal, and the dashboard retained an unsaved label draft. The companion resets local task rows transactionally; the dashboard keys task forms by generation. Pixoo and Tidbyt use their current snapshot reconciliation. No native mode change is added; Nanoleaf #110 owns presence-driven Work/Free automation. Existing skipped-revision comet suppression remains Nanoleaf #115.\n\nValidation completed locally: build/type; shared controller/lifecycle/state/MCP suites and package checks; Hub/setup/MCP/package checks; Tidbyt Node and Python; LIFX; dashboard browser/matrix/retirement checks; workflow checks; and the source harness against pinned Pixoo and the Nanoleaf companion, including 41 shared fixtures. The core contract passed an independent two-round task review after correcting archived-parent admission. Red tests established atomic removal, delayed-event protection, archive admission, Nanoleaf override retention and the dashboard draft gap. Guide inputs were refreshed and reconciled; generated output and maintenance/browser checks are recorded in the review receipt. OpenSpec inventory: 16 main specs, one active change, 25 archived changes; this change remains active.\n\nThe first independent Standards and Specification source reviews found no blockers. The user also authorized the companion repair for Nanoleaf #117: polling no longer steals focus from Open in Codex. Its deterministic browser regression failed before the fix and the complete browser suite passed afterward. This Hub candidate pins that updated consumer, and the owning-source harness passes all 41 shared fixtures. Both independent reviews found no actionable findings on the updated candidates: Hub `6acc74717092f0024cea53a0544207443a5c8c04` and Nanoleaf `d3ae501413782d86aa5b3fadbbe9462d594af655`.\n\nAll six current-head Hub jobs and all five companion jobs passed. The [review and CI receipt](https://github.com/jimmie-potts/agent-device-hub/pull/238#issuecomment-5821936547) identifies each check. The user approved the current dashboard and companion map behavior. This remains a draft pending the named installation/device trial and its acceptance evidence, including the issue's reopen-without-prompt observation. No installation, hook/Codex-file change, physical device operation or public guide publication occurred. Issue #218's throwaway archive/non-archive/resume trial and visible Nanoleaf Line timing remain open. Incomplete acceptance prevents OpenSpec archive, final review, merge and closure.\n\nAt the prior Hub head `8ba5ca5`, five of six hosted jobs passed; the Python 3.12 job failed with an empty failed-step log. Its cause remains unknown. Fresh local Tidbyt checks passed (72 Node and two Python tests). The current head gets fresh CI because it updates the companion source pin; no blind rerun or CI weakening was used.\n",
    "state": "open",
